.\" Manpage for tarpit-honeypot.
.\" Render: man ./docs/tarpit-honeypot.1   Install: cp to /usr/share/man/man1/
.TH TARPIT-HONEYPOT 1 "2026-06-08" "tarpit-honeypot 2.20.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
tarpit-honeypot \- CGO-free deception + detection honeypot sensor
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tarpit-honeypot
.RI [ global-flags ]
.I subcommand
.RI [ flags ]
.br
.B tarpit-honeypot
.BR --version | -V
.br
.B tarpit-honeypot
.BR help " [" \fIsubcommand\fR | \fItopic\fR ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B tarpit-honeypot
is a single, statically linked, CGO-free Go binary that stands up
plausible decoy services (SSH, HTTP/S, FTP, SMB, RDP, databases, a large
set of ICS/OT protocols, printers/MFDs, remote-access and out-of-band
management endpoints, the cloud-native control plane \(em Kubernetes API,
etcd, Vault, cloud instance metadata \(em and modern transports such as
WebSocket and gRPC), records every interaction as a structured event,
and forwards alerts to one or more outbound channels. It never exposes a
real shell, filesystem, or exploitable code path: dangerous operations are
recognized and logged, never performed.
.PP
An optional
.I archetype
pins every fingerprint-bearing handler to a single coherent device
identity (banners, SNMP identity, MAC OUI, TLS posture, ICMP TTL, web UI,
advertised CVEs) so a scanner cannot catch the sensor on an internal
inconsistency. See
.BR "tarpit-honeypot list-archetypes" " and " docs/archetypes.md .
.PP
Multiple sensors form a
.IR fleet :
a leaf
.RB ( forward: )
ships its events to an
.I aggregator
.RB ( ingest: ),
which runs the cross-sensor
.B fleet:*
correlator over the merged stream to surface patterns no single sensor
can see, derives indicators of compromise, and can serve them back down
as a threat-intel feed (STIX 2.1 / TAXII 2.1 / MISP). Events flow up;
intel flows down. See
.BR "tarpit-honeypot list-fleet-rules" ", " docs/fleet.md ", and " docs/ioc.md .
.PP
On a host with several IP addresses a wildcard listener answers on every
address (the destination IP is recorded per event); set
.B sensor.listen_addresses
to pin listeners to specific IPs. To run several independent honeypots on one
host \(em one per server IP, each with its own config, database, certificates
and UI \(em use the
.B tarpit-honeypot@.service
systemd template and
.BR scripts/add-instance.sh .
The web console adds per-service analytics pages (dns / web / databases /
remote / mail / ntp), an attacker-interactions view, attacker-intent on every
view, and a cross-target timeline on the source profile.
.SH GLOBAL FLAGS
.TP
.BR --version ", " -V
Print version, commit, and build date, then exit.
.TP
.BR -h ", " --help
Print the subcommand index.
.SH COMMON FLAGS
Most subcommands accept the shared logging and config-override set.
.TP
.BI -c " path"
Config file (default
.IR /etc/tarpit-honeypot/config.yaml ).
Repeatable to combine files.
.TP
.BI --set " key.path=value"
Override one YAML key (repeatable; type auto-detected). Applied last.
.TP
.BI -i ", " --interface " name"
Network interface for the AF_PACKET layer (repeatable).
.TP
.BI --log-level " level"
.BR debug | info | warn | error
(default
.BR info ).
.TP
.BI --log-format " fmt"
.BR text | json
(default
.BR text ).
.TP
.BI --log-file " path"
Redirect logs to a file (default stderr).
.TP
.BR --quiet ", " --verbose
Shortcuts for
.B --log-level=error
and
.BR --log-level=debug .
.SH SUBCOMMANDS
.TP
.B run
Start the sensor (listeners + sinks). Reloads config on
.BR SIGHUP ;
.B --dry-run
loads and validates without binding.
.TP
.B validate-config
Parse and validate a config; also reports TLS-cert \(em deployment-domain
coherence warnings. With
.B --diff
classify hot-reloadable vs restart-required changes between two configs.
.TP
.B show-config
Print the effective post-defaults config as YAML (secrets stay as env-var
names).
.TP
.B test-alert
Emit a synthetic event through the configured channels.
.TP
.B list-events
Tail / query events from the local SQLite store or JSON logs
.RB ( --since ", " --source ", " --target ", " --handler ", " --port ", " --rule ", " --fleet ", " --limit ).
.B --target
filters by which of the host's own server IPs was hit \(em the multi-IP pivot.
.TP
.B replay
Re-run a recorded request through its protocol handler offline (no socket,
nothing executed) and print the recomputed anomalies / CVE hits / meta.
.RB ( --event " " \fIid\fR " replays a stored event's captured payload and reports detection drift; " --handler " " \fIname\fR " + " --file / stdin " feeds arbitrary bytes; " --udp ", " --vuln ).
.TP
.B gen-token
Print or write an unguessable canary token
.RB ( --type " " dns | url | hex | ssrf | doc ).
The
.B ssrf
type emits a callback URL to plant in an SSRF parameter, webhook field, or
XXE entity; the
.B doc
type writes a decoy
.B .docx
.RB ( -o )
that beacons the callback URL when opened in Word. Point either at an
.B ssrf_canary
listener to record out-of-band fetches.
.TP
.B export-pcap
Copy one event's per-connection pcap out of the captures directory.
.TP
.B list-honeynets
Print the configured artificial networks, hosts, and services.
.TP
.B list-devices
Print the learned-device inventory
.RI ( devices.json );
.B --json
for a baseline snapshot.
.TP
.B list-archetypes
Print the device/OS archetype catalog; add a name for the full
fingerprint, or
.BR --json .
.TP
.B list-handlers
Print the protocol handlers valid in a listener
.RB ( --proto " " tcp | udp ).
.TP
.B list-cves
Print the CVE catalog used for emulation + exploit detection
.RB ( --service ", " --json ).
.TP
.B list-techniques
Print the MITRE ATT&CK / D3FEND coverage map
.RB ( --kind " " rule | handler | anomaly ).
.TP
.B export-navigator
Write a MITRE ATT&CK Navigator layer (JSON) to stdout or
.BR -o .
Default is static coverage; with
.B --observed
.RB ( -c ", " --since )
the layer is scored by per-technique event counts from the local store. Load it
at https://mitre-attack.github.io/attack-navigator/. The web UI also serves it
at /attack/navigator.
.TP
.B list-fleet-rules
Print the cross-sensor
.B fleet:*
detector catalog (id, what it detects, the
.B fleet.*
config keys that tune it);
.B --json
for machine-readable output.
.TP
.B export-iocs
Derive indicators of compromise from the stored
.B fleet:*
findings and print them for a TIP / SIEM / blocklist
.RB ( --format " " stix | csv | txt | json ", " --since ).
.TP
.B identify
Run the offline fingerprint engine over a supplied User-Agent, path, SSH
client id, JA3, or payload
.RB ( --ua ", " --ssh ", " --ja3 ", " --payload ", " --handler ).
.TP
.B jarm \fIhost:port\fR
Actively probe a TLS server and print its JARM fingerprint
.RB ( --timeout ", " --sni ).
Point it at the sensor's own
.B https
listener to confirm its TLS posture matches the impersonated product.
.TP
.B generate-decoys \fItarget...\fR
Scan your own network (host, host:port, or CIDR) and emit a matching
tarpit-honeypot listener config so the honeypot blends into the environment. A
benign TCP connect scanner — no exploit traffic, no stealth scanning, no writes
to the target. Requires
.B --yes
to confirm authorization; bounded by
.RB ( --ports ", " --timeout ", " --concurrency ", " --max-hosts ", " --no-banners ", " --bind ", " --archetype ", " -o ).
Review and
.B validate-config
the output before deploying.
.TP
.B init
Write a default config skeleton to stdout (or
.BR -o ).
.TP
.B completion
Print a shell-completion script
.RB ( bash | zsh | fish ).
.TP
.B help
Long-form help for a subcommand or topic
.RB ( "help archetypes" ).
.TP
.B version
Print version info.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/tarpit-honeypot/config.yaml
Default configuration file.
.TP
.I <data_dir>/events.db
SQLite event store (powers
.BR list-events ", " export-iocs ,
and the web UI; also holds the de-duplicated IOC store and the persisted
ATT&CK / D3FEND attribution).
.TP
.I <data_dir>/sensor_id
Stable per-deployment id (survives hostname changes; the fleet
dedup/attribution key).
.TP
.I <data_dir>/devices.json
Learned-device inventory.
.TP
.I <data_dir>/captures/<event_id>.pcap
Per-connection packet captures (when
.B capture: true
is set on a listener).
.TP
.I <data_dir>/payloads/<sha256>.<ext>
Harvested malware / payload artifacts (when
.B sensor.payloads
is enabled).
.TP
.I <data_dir>/sessions/<name>.cast
Recorded interactive-shell sessions (asciicast).
.PP
The captured malware, pcaps, session recordings, and credentials are all
retrievable from the web UI's
.B captures
tab (served as download-only attachments).
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Validate a config and start the sensor:
.PP
.RS
.nf
tarpit-honeypot validate-config -c /etc/tarpit-honeypot/config.yaml
tarpit-honeypot run -c /etc/tarpit-honeypot/config.yaml
.fi
.RE
.PP
Inspect the built-in catalogs offline:
.PP
.RS
.nf
tarpit-honeypot list-archetypes xerox-versalink
tarpit-honeypot list-cves --service ipp
tarpit-honeypot list-techniques --kind anomaly
.fi
.RE
.PP
Triage a captured signal:
.PP
.RS
.nf
tarpit-honeypot identify --ua "sqlmap/1.7" --payload '${jndi:ldap://x/a}'
.fi
.RE
.SH SECURITY
The sensor is read-only by design and never becomes an
amplification/reflection primitive or an exploitable target. The one
opt-in write path (web-UI device delete/whitelist) is gated by a config
flag plus an auth token. Run it as an unprivileged user; use
.B sensor.drop_user
/
.B sensor.drop_group
to drop privileges after binding low ports.
